const sourceDeep={
6:{title:'Erythrocyte shapes, inclusions and infectious agents',source:'LMU CP6 · Harvey · 98 slides',sections:[
['Start with definitions','Discocyte = mature biconcave mammalian red cell. Anisocytosis = variation in RBC size; poikilocytosis = variation in shape; macrocyte/microcyte = larger/smaller than species norms; polychromatophil = bluish immature RBC on Wright stain. Normal camelid erythrocytes are oval. Goats and young cattle may have physiologic poikilocytosis.'],
['Membrane shape: echinocyte vs acanthocyte','Echinocyte = regular short evenly distributed spicules; often drying, storage or EDTA artifact, but also reported with uremia, envenomation and disorders of ionic balance. Acanthocyte = irregular projections with varying lengths; altered membrane lipids and microvascular injury can accompany canine hemangiosarcoma, hepatic disease or DIC. Species and smear preparation matter.'],
['Rouleaux vs agglutination','Rouleaux = stacked coins, particularly normal in horses and sometimes cats, enhanced by plasma proteins. Agglutination = irregular antibody-linked RBC aggregates that tend to persist after saline dilution. The lecture describes a 1:49 blood:saline comparison; interpret with fresh smear and appropriate method.'],
['Spherocytes: pathogenesis','A spherocyte is a smaller-diameter, dense RBC with absent central pallor because membrane surface area was lost, often after macrophage partial phagocytosis of antibody/complement-coated cells. Its volume need not be low. Large numbers can support IMHA in dogs, but fragmentation, envenomation, zinc and stored transfusion blood are alternatives; harder to recognize in cats.'],
['Schistocytes and fragmentation','Schistocyte = sheared red cell fragment (often triangular or helmet-like). Strong evidence of mechanical fragmentation; other associated forms include keratocytes and acanthocytes. Evaluate DIC, vessel/endothelial injury, hemangiosarcoma, severe burns, heartworm caval syndrome and severe iron deficiency in context. A few small sphere-like cells may also appear with fragmentation, without proving IMHA.'],
['Oxidative damage','Eccentrocyte = hemoglobin pushed to one side, creating a clear crescent after membrane oxidant injury. Pyknocytes can develop from damaged cells. Heinz bodies = precipitated oxidized hemoglobin, best seen with supravital stain; low numbers can be normal in cats. Compare with Howell–Jolly bodies (dark DNA nuclear remnant) and basophilic stippling (aggregated ribosomal RNA).'],
['Nucleated RBCs','Metarubricytes/rubricytes outside marrow may accompany regeneration, splenic dysfunction, lead toxicity, heatstroke, severe inflammation or marrow injury/neoplasia. Their presence alone does not establish regeneration: pair with absolute reticulocytes and patient context.'],
['Erythrocyte infections','Review Babesia (including B. bigemina cattle and B. gibsoni dogs), Theileria equi horses, Cytauxzoon felis cats, Anaplasma marginale cattle, and hemotropic mycoplasmas such as M. haemofelis. Compare location on vs inside RBC, species, clinical consequences, and sampling artifacts; morphology should be corroborated with species-appropriate tests.']
],photos:[['Normal mammalian and camelid blood',3,5],['Rouleaux vs agglutination',14,18],['Echinocytes and acanthocytes',29,36],['Spherocyte and schistocyte',43,49],['Eccentrocyte and inclusions',58,75],['Babesia and hemoplasmas',81,96]]},
8:{title:'Laboratory evaluation of anemias',source:'LMU CP8 · Harvey · 68 slides',sections:[
['Start with the definition','Anemia means reduced circulating erythrocyte mass reflected by low HCT/PCV and usually low Hb/RBC count relative to species and physiology. It is a finding, NOT a cause-specific diagnosis. HCT is fraction of blood volume occupied by RBCs; total plasma protein (TPP) helps contextualize dilution and loss.'],
['Blood sample and patient','Assess prior drugs/toxins, age/onset, hemorrhage and transfusion. Look for pallor, tachycardia, weakness, icterus or hemoglobinuria. Inspect microhematocrit plasma for icterus (yellow), hemolysis (red/pink), lipemia (opaque) and buffy coat.'],
['HCT x total plasma protein','Low HCT + low TPP supports blood loss or fluid dilution; low HCT + normal TPP may accompany destruction, diminished production or chronic hemorrhage; low HCT + high TPP suggests considering inflammatory/hyperglobulinemic conditions. These are patterns, not diagnoses.'],
['Hemodilution vs true anemia','Expansion of plasma volume can lower concentration without equivalent RBC loss. Lecture examples include pregnancy (not horses), neonatal growth, overhydration and splenic sequestration with anesthesia. Interpret PCV with hydration, drugs and species.'],
['Regenerative vs nonregenerative','A regenerative anemia has an increased marrow response, judged by absolute reticulocyte count and polychromasia in dogs/cats, and sometimes marrow evaluation in horses. Anemia due to hemorrhage or destruction may initially lack reticulocytosis because an increased response needs ~3–4 days.'],
['Reticulocyte definitions and feline trap','Reticulocyte = immature anucleate RBC containing residual ribosomal RNA. Aggregate reticulocyte has coarse clumped RNA and reflects recent release; punctate reticulocyte has fine residual dots and survives longer in cats. Use aggregate reticulocytes to assess current feline regeneration. Horses and ruminants normally show little or no circulating reticulocyte response.'],
['Calculate absolute reticulocytes','Absolute reticulocytes/µL = RBC count/µL × retic fraction. Lecture slide 57–58 example: anemic dog RBC 2.3 million/µL × 3% = 69,000/µL; healthy dog RBC 6.9 million/µL × 1% = ALSO 69,000/µL. The higher percentage alone does not mean better regeneration.'],
['MCV and MCHC','MCV = mean corpuscular volume (average RBC size); macrocytosis often accompanies marked reticulocytosis. MCHC = mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (Hb concentration within red cells); hypochromasia can occur with iron-limited erythropoiesis. Routine indices are averages and change slower than reticulocyte-specific hemoglobin measurements (RETIC-HGB).'],
['Interpret species and time','Cats: aggregate reticulocytes mature into punctate within a day, while punctate can persist a week or more. Dogs: blood reticulocytes mature in about a day. Horses generally do not circulate reticulocytes after anemia, so examine marrow/regenerative indices differently. Ruminant response is relatively slow.'],
['Analyzer traps','Automated reticulocyte signals may be confused by nucleated RBCs, Howell–Jolly bodies, RBC parasites, Heinz bodies and platelet clumps; reconcile plots and smear. Evaluate RETIC-HGB for early iron-limited erythropoiesis only within validated instrument/species context.']
],photos:[['PCV, plasma and sample appearance',7,9],['Regenerative vs nonregenerative smear',66,67],['Reticulocytes on Wright and supravital stains',25,30],['Feline reticulocytes and agglutination',32,34],['Reticulocyte interpretation graph',52,58]]}
};

